WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.634 --> 00:00:02.902
 Phil, John, congratulations.

00:00:02.969 --> 00:00:04.537
You guys have landed
in the final round

00:00:04.604 --> 00:00:05.872
of this competition.

00:00:05.939 --> 00:00:07.574
Now we're sending you
back to your home forges

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:11.111
to recreate this iconic
weapon from history.

00:00:14.381 --> 00:00:15.148
The partisan.

00:00:15.248 --> 00:00:17.116
 [gasps]

00:00:17.183 --> 00:00:18.751
 Good luck, bladesmiths.

00:00:18.851 --> 00:00:21.854
We'll see you in four days.

00:00:21.921 --> 00:00:23.289 line:7%
 Today's day 1.

00:00:23.355 --> 00:00:25.558 line:0%
And the main thing I want
to get accomplished today

00:00:25.625 --> 00:00:27.127 line:7%
is forging out that socket.

00:00:27.193 --> 00:00:29.662
If I don't get that done, that's
going to put me behind where

00:00:29.729 --> 00:00:30.796
I need to be for tomorrow.

00:00:30.863 --> 00:00:31.764
What do you think of that?

00:00:31.864 --> 00:00:33.065
 Why does it have a hole?

00:00:33.165 --> 00:00:35.668
 Because the spear
is going to go inside.

00:00:35.734 --> 00:00:39.439
 For the blade, I'm starting
out with a good chunk of sheet

00:00:39.539 --> 00:00:41.741
steel, 1095 high carbon.

00:00:41.841 --> 00:00:43.509
There we go.

00:00:43.609 --> 00:00:46.745
I am overcome with joy
with the amount of progress

00:00:46.812 --> 00:00:47.547
that I've made today.

00:00:50.516 --> 00:00:53.185
 First thing we got to do is
get the profile established,

00:00:53.252 --> 00:00:56.055
get our medial ridge ground
in, take a lot of the weight

00:00:56.121 --> 00:00:58.491
out, and get it
ready for heat treat.

00:00:58.591 --> 00:01:00.226
A weapon like this
is meant to be swung,

00:01:00.292 --> 00:01:01.227
so it needs to be well balanced.

00:01:01.294 --> 00:01:02.996
It can't be too top heavy.

00:01:03.063 --> 00:01:05.231
But I need to balance that
with strength as well.

00:01:05.298 --> 00:01:07.333 line:0%
So the plan for heat
treating the blade

00:01:07.399 --> 00:01:09.935 line:0%
is to clay up each blade
individually and differentially

00:01:10.002 --> 00:01:11.337 line:0%
harden the edges
so that it stays

00:01:11.403 --> 00:01:13.839 line:0%
nice and soft and
springy, and only

00:01:13.939 --> 00:01:15.174 line:7%
our cutting edge gets hard.

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:16.975 line:0%
If the whole blade was
as hard as the edge,

00:01:17.076 --> 00:01:18.978 line:0%
it would be really
likely to break.

00:01:19.078 --> 00:01:20.146
All righty.

00:01:26.152 --> 00:01:27.287
Well, it's straight.

00:01:27.387 --> 00:01:28.688
Now we're going to
clay and heat treat

00:01:28.788 --> 00:01:30.090
the flanges one at a time.

00:01:34.126 --> 00:01:35.361
Last quench right here.

00:01:38.398 --> 00:01:40.099
Hell yeah.

00:01:40.199 --> 00:01:43.136
Thing's hard as woodpecker lips.

00:01:43.203 --> 00:01:44.703
 Today is day 2.

00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:46.673
And the plan for today is
just to get that medial ridge

00:01:46.773 --> 00:01:48.208
situated just right.

00:01:48.274 --> 00:01:51.143
And what I've got to figure
out is how exactly to do this.

00:01:51.244 --> 00:01:53.580
Smiths have been making
medial ridges for hundreds

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:54.981
if not thousands of years.

00:01:55.047 --> 00:01:56.883
But in all of my
research that I found,

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:58.317
I could not find how
they traditionally

00:01:58.417 --> 00:02:01.587
put medial ridges on blades
that were so pronounced.

00:02:01.654 --> 00:02:04.023
So I'm really having to go
just a little different route

00:02:04.123 --> 00:02:06.426
than I think what is
traditionally done.

00:02:06.492 --> 00:02:08.761
I'm going to take bar
stock, tack weld it,

00:02:08.827 --> 00:02:12.498
throw it in the forge, forge
weld it, throw a bead of weld,

00:02:12.598 --> 00:02:14.933
and then I'm going to go in and
clean up those welds so I get

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:17.536
those really drastic curves.

00:02:17.603 --> 00:02:20.139
The weight of the piece
is going to be heavier,

00:02:20.206 --> 00:02:22.675
but I'm going to have
structural integrity.

00:02:22.742 --> 00:02:24.477
That is sufficiently ground.

00:02:24.577 --> 00:02:26.945
I think we are
ready to heat treat.

00:02:27.046 --> 00:02:29.516
[music playing]

00:02:36.823 --> 00:02:38.957
I think we are good.

00:02:39.024 --> 00:02:40.125
 It's day 4.

00:02:40.192 --> 00:02:41.427
The sun's finally out.

00:02:41.494 --> 00:02:44.330
Today's mostly refining
work, fit and finish,

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:46.132
getting everything
cleaned up, sharpened,

00:02:46.198 --> 00:02:48.901
and getting the sphere mounted.

00:02:49.001 --> 00:02:52.771
So now I'm forging a nail to
affix the sphere to the shaft.

00:02:52.838 --> 00:02:54.674
So I've just got a piece of
square stock, taper it down.

00:02:54.774 --> 00:02:56.209
We're going to cut it
off on the parent bar

00:02:56.309 --> 00:02:57.810
and chuck it up
with a nail header.

00:02:57.876 --> 00:02:59.078
There's our nail.

00:02:59.144 --> 00:03:00.146
I could do better.

00:03:00.212 --> 00:03:02.982
See how the head's off center?

00:03:03.082 --> 00:03:04.450
I don't know this one.

00:03:04.516 --> 00:03:05.818
It's going to be inside--
you're not going to know.

00:03:05.885 --> 00:03:06.819
It's fine.

00:03:06.919 --> 00:03:07.554
Oh, god, I'll know, though.

00:03:10.355 --> 00:03:11.457
I'm going to make another one.

00:03:11.524 --> 00:03:15.228
[rock music]

00:03:15.294 --> 00:03:16.863
That one's better.

00:03:16.963 --> 00:03:18.131
What my spear
brings to the table

00:03:18.231 --> 00:03:20.934
is the good blend of
form and function.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:22.168
And with the way I
made it, it should

00:03:22.234 --> 00:03:24.470
be pretty much indestructible.

00:03:24.537 --> 00:03:25.771
Feels good.

00:03:25.838 --> 00:03:30.643
 The plan for today is to
clean up the surface grind,

00:03:30.743 --> 00:03:32.345
get everything nice
and shiny, then

00:03:32.445 --> 00:03:35.615
continue on with the bevels,
and then start assembling.

00:03:35.715 --> 00:03:37.483
I'm just going to
kind of do a dry fit

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:43.055
and see where I'm at
with the pole here.

00:03:43.122 --> 00:03:44.591
Not going to peen it.

00:03:44.691 --> 00:03:46.325
I still need to take it all
apart for some more work,

00:03:46.425 --> 00:03:49.362
but just getting that step
in the process done and ready

00:03:49.462 --> 00:03:51.930
to move forward with it.

00:03:51.997 --> 00:03:53.266
Whoops.

00:03:53.332 --> 00:03:54.500
I'm thinking, yeah,
it'll come right off.

00:03:54.567 --> 00:03:55.301
No problem.

00:03:57.836 --> 00:04:00.340
I was wrong.

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:02.441
It's like, why am I taking
this out if it fits so well?

00:04:02.541 --> 00:04:05.478
But to keep working
on it, I need it out.

00:04:05.578 --> 00:04:06.512
Wow.

00:04:06.613 --> 00:04:07.679
I'm an idiot.

00:04:07.746 --> 00:04:09.515
[grunts]

00:04:11.851 --> 00:04:13.286
That's not budging.

00:04:13.353 --> 00:04:17.657
As I'm prying on this vise,
trying to get the bar out,

00:04:17.724 --> 00:04:21.627
it dawns on me,
heat up the metal.

00:04:21.694 --> 00:04:22.862
Burn the wood.

00:04:22.962 --> 00:04:25.564
It'll come right out.

00:04:25.631 --> 00:04:27.033
Voila.

00:04:27.099 --> 00:04:29.202
Disaster averted.

00:04:29.302 --> 00:04:31.236
Of all the things
to go wrong, right?

00:04:31.303 --> 00:04:34.473
A major build,
$10,000 on the line.

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:39.178
He prematurely gets his
wooden staff stuck in it.

00:04:39.244 --> 00:04:41.246
So I'm not planning
on testing right now.

00:04:41.313 --> 00:04:42.615
If it's going to
malfunction, if it's

00:04:42.715 --> 00:04:44.517
going to break at this
point, I want the judges

00:04:44.617 --> 00:04:47.119
to have the honor of destroying
something that I've built,

00:04:47.186 --> 00:04:48.120
not me.

00:04:48.187 --> 00:04:49.855
I think that feels good.

00:04:49.922 --> 00:04:51.123
 All right, bladesmiths.

00:04:51.190 --> 00:04:54.560
This is the kill test.

00:04:54.626 --> 00:04:56.462
To test the lethality
of your weapon,

00:04:56.529 --> 00:04:59.098
I will deliver killing blows
on this ballistics dummy.

00:04:59.198 --> 00:05:00.032
Phil, you're up first.

00:05:00.099 --> 00:05:00.800
You ready for this?

00:05:00.867 --> 00:05:01.534
 Yes, sir, man.

00:05:01.634 --> 00:05:02.634
Let's do it.

00:05:02.734 --> 00:05:04.269
Going into this test,
my heart's starting

00:05:04.336 --> 00:05:05.838
to pump a little faster.

00:05:05.905 --> 00:05:08.640
Doug's hopefully just going to
destroy this ballistics dummy,

00:05:08.707 --> 00:05:11.043
and we're going to have pieces
flying all over the place

00:05:11.143 --> 00:05:14.846
and blood on the walls.

00:05:14.913 --> 00:05:18.451
[rock music]

00:05:29.895 --> 00:05:31.430
 All right, Phil.

00:05:31.497 --> 00:05:35.468
The design you
have here is scary.

00:05:35.535 --> 00:05:38.871
That's a very thick spine you
have, and the edges are sharp.

00:05:38.938 --> 00:05:41.307
As you can see, very deadly.

00:05:41.373 --> 00:05:46.712
The one thing about
this is that it's heavy.

00:05:46.778 --> 00:05:48.714
That is something to take into
account when you're carrying

00:05:48.780 --> 00:05:50.148
something like this
into battle and you've

00:05:50.215 --> 00:05:52.218
got to move multiple times.

00:05:52.285 --> 00:05:56.154
But more importantly,
your weapon will kill.

00:05:56.255 --> 00:05:57.489
 Thank you, sir

00:05:57.556 --> 00:05:58.857
 All right, John.
Your turn.

00:05:58.924 --> 00:05:59.625
You ready?

00:05:59.726 --> 00:06:00.793
 Let's do it.

00:06:00.893 --> 00:06:02.595
I'm feeling good
about the kill test.

00:06:02.695 --> 00:06:07.132
I know my blade is sharp, and
I think it'll cut really well.

00:06:07.232 --> 00:06:10.737
[rock music]

00:06:21.747 --> 00:06:24.050
 [laughs]

00:06:26.218 --> 00:06:29.054
Pushed out a rib.

00:06:29.155 --> 00:06:30.156
All right, John.

00:06:30.222 --> 00:06:31.590
I love the feel of the blade.

00:06:31.657 --> 00:06:33.525
You can really fight with
it because it's lighter.

00:06:33.592 --> 00:06:37.130
Your blade also is very
slim that it penetrates

00:06:37.196 --> 00:06:39.098
in there when there's
no resistance,

00:06:39.164 --> 00:06:41.134
even enough to push out a rib.

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:44.870
More important, I love the
balance, I love the feel,

00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:46.071
and it will kill.

00:06:46.172 --> 00:06:46.939
 Thank you, sir.

00:06:49.942 --> 00:06:52.544
 All right, gentlemen, it's
time for the strength test--

00:06:52.644 --> 00:06:56.949
the pike, push, and armor stab.

00:06:57.016 --> 00:07:01.354
Now, the partisan was often used
in castle or in city defense,

00:07:01.421 --> 00:07:03.122
so to test the overall
strength and construction

00:07:03.189 --> 00:07:05.557
of your blades, I'll be
pushing against these pikes

00:07:05.624 --> 00:07:07.893
with the blade and then
stabbing into the armor.

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:11.063
Now, this test is not about what
your weapons do to the targets.

00:07:11.163 --> 00:07:14.600
It's about what those targets
are going to do your weapons.

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:15.801
Phillip, you're up first.
You ready?

00:07:15.868 --> 00:07:16.636
 Yes, sir.

00:07:26.044 --> 00:07:29.515
[rock music]

00:07:42.662 --> 00:07:43.996
 All right, Phillip.

00:07:44.096 --> 00:07:46.398
This thing is a beast.

00:07:46.465 --> 00:07:48.567
The weight, the
shape of your tip

00:07:48.633 --> 00:07:50.836
really makes it a good
penetrating weapon.

00:07:50.936 --> 00:07:53.371
The issue I have is the
partisan was a standoff weapon.

00:07:53.472 --> 00:07:55.508
You got to be able
to control it.

00:07:55.575 --> 00:07:59.078
And at full extension, that
weight is hard to control.

00:07:59.178 --> 00:08:02.548
I like the fact that you
have this high medial ridge.

00:08:02.615 --> 00:08:04.083
I like your design.

00:08:04.183 --> 00:08:05.317
My only issue is the weight.

00:08:05.384 --> 00:08:06.017
Good job.

00:08:06.118 --> 00:08:07.253
 Thank you, sir.

00:08:07.319 --> 00:08:08.754
 John, you ready?

00:08:08.855 --> 00:08:10.555
 Let's do it.

00:08:10.622 --> 00:08:12.224
I am a little bit
worried about the blade

00:08:12.324 --> 00:08:13.759
going into the strength test.

00:08:13.826 --> 00:08:16.462
Stabbing at armor with the
blade being as thin as it is,

00:08:16.529 --> 00:08:18.564
I was really concerned about
keeping the weight down.

00:08:21.400 --> 00:08:23.469
Hopefully it holds up.

00:08:23.569 --> 00:08:27.106
[rock music]

00:08:40.419 --> 00:08:41.053
[bleep]

00:08:43.555 --> 00:08:46.325
 OK, John, the weapon is light.

00:08:46.392 --> 00:08:47.660
It's well balanced.

00:08:47.727 --> 00:08:48.694
I really like that.

00:08:48.760 --> 00:08:51.030
The issue I have is down here.

00:08:51.096 --> 00:08:55.000
It looks as if this
is the thinnest spot.

00:08:55.067 --> 00:08:57.303
Now, you've got a
differential heat treat.

00:08:57.403 --> 00:08:58.971
You can actually
see those lines.

00:08:59.038 --> 00:09:02.041
And that soft core may be
what's affecting this blade.

00:09:02.141 --> 00:09:05.645
So as soon as I stabbed in and
it locked up, that blade went.

00:09:05.711 --> 00:09:08.914
With this bend, it's not safe
to test this blade anymore.

00:09:09.014 --> 00:09:10.783
It's going to keep on
bending, and it's going to get

00:09:10.849 --> 00:09:12.184
harder and harder to control.

00:09:15.020 --> 00:09:18.357
 John, your blade has suffered
a significant deformation

00:09:18.424 --> 00:09:19.992
in the course of
our strength test.

00:09:20.059 --> 00:09:23.662
It's received a vote of no
confidence from the judges.

00:09:23.728 --> 00:09:26.031
I have to ask you to
please leave the forge.

00:09:26.131 --> 00:09:28.667
 I completely agree with
the judge's decision.

00:09:28.733 --> 00:09:30.269
With the way that blade
bent, I wouldn't be

00:09:30.335 --> 00:09:31.336
comfortable testing it either.

00:09:31.403 --> 00:09:32.237
 Good job.

00:09:32.338 --> 00:09:33.506
 Awesome job.

00:09:33.606 --> 00:09:34.873
Seriously.

00:09:34.974 --> 00:09:36.509
 Even though I'm not a
"Forged in Fire" champion,

00:09:36.575 --> 00:09:38.410
I am proud of the
work I turned in.

00:09:38.476 --> 00:09:40.079
Phil, congratulations, brother.

00:09:40.145 --> 00:09:43.583
You did a great job,
and I'm happy for you.

00:09:43.649 --> 00:09:47.186
 Phillip, you are the new
"Forged in Fire" champion.

00:09:47.252 --> 00:09:49.354
That's a title that comes
with a check for $10,000.

00:09:49.421 --> 00:09:50.088
Good job.

00:09:50.155 --> 00:09:52.625
[applause]

00:09:52.691 --> 00:09:54.893
 I just won "Forged in Fire."

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:58.830
John, you're an awesome
dude, an amazing competitor.

00:09:58.897 --> 00:10:01.433
The sense of joy
and accomplishment,

00:10:01.500 --> 00:10:02.968
knowing my daughter
is going to see,

00:10:03.035 --> 00:10:04.636
there's so many
emotions right now.

00:10:04.703 --> 00:10:06.238
There's-- I'm overwhelmed.

00:10:06.339 --> 00:10:08.807
I'm trying to hold
back tears, man.

00:10:08.874 --> 00:10:10.876
I'm taking it home to Texas.

00:10:10.977 --> 00:10:14.146
[music playing]

